// Split out for #5229: same module as stwwiring.bit (§17.1) — a directory is
// its own Bit module, so Edge, extendEdges and tMacos are already declared or
// imported there and re-declaring any of them here would be E0042.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT `assert giveups == 0`. #5228's acceptance
// named a runtime test reading `bit_rt_port_gc_world_resume_giveup` (the
// `giveups=` BIT_GC_STATS field) and asserting it stays 0. #5229's 12-shape
// bound-scan campaign (see that ticket's comments) measured why that can never
// be written: `stwParkBound` (`../../../runtime/gc/gcworld.bit`, 2^32) is
// sized so a legitimately long mark/sweep cannot reach it, and the largest
// shape built — an 18-mutator, 2.5 GB live heap, 4.55-6.65 s genuine
// stop-the-world with every mutator's park within 20 ms of every other —
// still produced 0 give-ups. A scan that shrank the bound to 1/10 and 1/100
// of real produced 103 and 972, so the give-up branch DOES fire; it is a
// wedge detector (`worldRestart` never comes), not a contention counter a
// realistic workload can reach. Asserting 0 on it would pass by construction,
// never by the property holding — the same vacuity this file's header already
// refuses for every other check here.
//
// The deterministic regression for `37f00152` (the fix `#5228`'s stats wiring
// was built to observe) is structural instead, and #5750 moved WHICH primitive
// it names without changing what it proves. The waiting mutator must still
// reach an OS-level "stop burning this core" call past its first burst; that
// call is no longer `worldYield()`.
//
// WHY IT MOVED. #5748 made the tail yield on EVERY check rather than one in a
// thousand and measured the result: 44239 M -> 22135 M instructions on
// `bench/cases/allocpar` (21/21 reps) and CYCLES UNCHANGED (22468 M -> 22457 M,
// per-rep sign 9/21, identical to the byte-identical null control's own 9/21).
// A yielding thread still owns its core. #5750 therefore replaced the yield
// with a BLOCKING wait on the stop word itself
// (`../../../runtime/gc/gcstopwait.bit`, `__ulock_wait` on Darwin), woken by
// `worldRestart`. Both halves are edges here because a wait with no wake is a
// hang and a wake with no wait is a wasted syscall on every collection of every
// program — the two only mean anything as a pair.
//
// THE FIRST BURST IS UNTOUCHED BY ALL THREE TICKETS, which is what keeps #2415's
// "a mutator that acks within the first burst never reaches an OS call at all"
// true; these edges pin the tail, not the burst.
//
// MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED, ON THIS BRANCH. `bit build runtime/gc --emit-obj
// --freestanding --target aarch64-macos` places `_worldWaitForStop` at
// 0x4250 (next symbol `_worldResumeWhenClear` at 0x42c8) with a `BR26`
// relocation to `_bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wait` at 0x42a0 — inside that span —
// and `_bit_rt_port_gc_world_restart` at 0x47c4 (next symbol
// `_bit_rt_port_gc_world_lock` at 0x4820) with a `BR26` to
// `_bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wake` at 0x480c, reached only through the
// `cbnz` on the waiter-count load at 0x47ec. `worldResumeWhenClear` calls
// `worldWaitForStop` rather than inlining it (its own atom survives at 120
// bytes), so the wait edge is anchored on the helper; `worldRendezvous`'s own
// `bit_rt_port_gc_world_yield` edge at 0x4798 is untouched by #5750 and is
// deliberately NOT claimed here.
//
// `path: "runtime/gc"` (not a `darwin`/`linux` subdirectory) because every atom
// named here lives in a cross-platform file (`gcworldstop.bit`,
// `gcstopwait.bit`); both externs stay UNDEFINED in this freestanding object
// regardless of target — the same seam `gcmark.bit`'s header documents for
// `bit_rt_gc_mark_stack_overflow` — so one macOS build is enough to see the
// edges, and a second Linux build would exercise the identical cross-platform
// source.
const whyResumeWait: string = "worldResumeWhenClear's tail must still reach an OS-level wait past the first burst, or a real stop-the-world burns every waiting mutator's core for a whole mark and sweep; #5750 made that wait a blocking one on worldStopWord (bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wait) because #5748 measured the yield it replaces as instruction-only - 22.1 G instructions removed, cycles flat at the null control's own per-rep sign"

// The wake half. Deleting the `bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wake` call in
// `worldRestart` leaves every sleeping mutator to its `stwWaitTimeoutUs`
// timeout instead of resuming when the stop clears, which is a latency
// regression no timing assertion in this suite would attribute correctly.
const whyRestartWake: string = "worldRestart must wake the waiters worldResumeWhenClear parks on worldStopWord (bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wake), gated on the waiter count so an uncontended restart still costs one load and a not-taken branch rather than a syscall - #5722's shape, where a mechanism sized for the workload being imagined was 5000x wrong on the shortest real one"

// The two edges. Delete the `worldWaitForStop(world)` call in
// `runtime/gc/gcworldstop.bit`'s `worldResumeWhenClear`, or the wake in
// `worldRestart` -> red.
fn worldResumeYieldEdges(): []Edge {
  return [
    Edge{
      path = "runtime/gc",
      target = tMacos,
      forbid = false,
      fromSym = "worldWaitForStop",
      toSym = "bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wait",
      why = whyResumeWait,
    },
    Edge{
      path = "runtime/gc",
      target = tMacos,
      forbid = false,
      fromSym = "bit_rt_port_gc_world_restart",
      toSym = "bit_rt_port_gc_world_stop_wake",
      why = whyRestartWake,
    },
  ]
}
